Structs are C's answer to classes like Java
If you want the speed of C but the organization of Java, structs are a good compromise. Structs are a way to group together different data types into one unit. For example, if you wanted to represent a point in 2D space, you could use a struct to group together two integers (x and y coordinates) into one unit.

C++ is a good alternative to Java if you still want the speed of C.

typedef struct{
    long_string_t label; // name for the point
    double x; // xcoordinate
    double y; // ycoordinate
}   point_t; // pick a name that suggests it is a stuct
point_t origin;
point_t focus;

